     1	# voice_echo
     2	
     3	On-demand raw PCM record-then-playback test application for the contest board.
     4	
     5	```text
     6	nsh> voice_echo
     7	nsh> voice_echo --once
     8	nsh> voice_echo recordmem
     9	vela> voice_echo recordmem
    10	```
    11	
    12	The default and `--once` commands immediately record exactly five seconds from
    13	I2S0 RX, play the captured PCM once through I2S1 TX, and return to NSH.
    14	`recordmem` records at most three seconds and enters ASR directly. GPIO14 is not connected
    15	on the current contest board, so this application does not use GPIO14, GPIO0,
    16	or `/dev/buttons`.
    17	
    18	OLED state transitions are best-effort. Playback uses `REC 5 SEC` -> `PLAY` ->
    19	`DONE`; `recordmem` uses `REC 3 SEC` -> `ASR REQ` -> `ASR DONE`.
    20	OLED failures are reported but do not abort audio.
    21	
    22	Audio profile: 16 kHz input, 24 kHz output, mono, 16-bit samples in 16-bit
    23	slots. The rates come from `eda-robot-pro/config.h`; sample width, slot width,
    24	and external module behavior are provisional and not hardware-validated.
    25	The record buffer is derived as `16000 * 5 * sizeof(int16_t)` (160000 bytes).
    26	Playback performs the existing 16 kHz to 24 kHz 3:2 conversion in DMA-sized
    27	chunks, so it does not allocate a second complete playback buffer.
    28	
    29	`recordmem` is the board-side voice-pipeline stage. It records at most three
    30	seconds of the same 16 kHz mono PCM, builds a standard 44-byte WAV header in
    31	RAM, and immediately submits the PCM buffer to the registered ASR backend. No
    32	path argument, filesystem, `fopen`, or `fwrite` is used. The WAV header is kept
    33	as an in-memory view for the next audio stage; the ASR interface receives the
    34	PCM portion because its contract is 16-bit LE, 16 kHz, mono PCM.
    35	
    36	When `CONFIG_EXAMPLES_AI_AGENT_VELA` is enabled, the `vela>` CLI exposes the
    37	same `voice_echo recordmem` command and calls the application in-process. It
    38	therefore reuses the ai_agent voice backend and credentials configured in
    39	`vela>`. The default backend is MiMo; configure its key with
    40	`set_mimo_key <api_key>` or `set_llm ... <api_key>`, then the command performs
    41	ASR, pushes the recognized text to the common agent message bus, and lets the
    42	agent produce the LLM/TTS response through the normal voice channel. The
    43	standalone `nsh>` command still reports `ASR unavailable` when ai_agent is
    44	disabled.
